# A deployment's own settings, read at start from `services.gtd-mcp.configFile`
# — /data/config.env on a cattle container. Copy it onto the state volume and
# edit; it is not part of the image and never in git.
#
# The published image is generic: this file is what makes it *this* vault's
# server, so changing vault, branch or caller is a restart rather than a
# rebuild. It holds no secrets — GTD_PUSH_TOKEN and GTD_WEBHOOK_SECRET belong
# in the secrets file beside it.
#
# ⚠️ Only settings that vary between deployments are honoured here. The vault
# path, the bind address and the port are the image's own: the unit's sandbox
# and firewall are built around them, so overriding them here only breaks it.

# The vault repo this server holds a clone of, and pushes its commits back to.
GTD_REMOTE_URL=https://gitlab.com/you/gtd.git
GTD_BRANCH=master

# Every tool call must carry an X-Auth-User header matching one of these,
# re-checked per call rather than only at an authenticating proxy. Comma
# separated. Empty means no check — right only if nothing untrusted can reach
# the port, which is not true of anything an ingress publishes.
GTD_ALLOWED_USERS=your-login

# What the unit refuses to start without. The image's own list is the three
# above plus the push token; add GTD_WEBHOOK_SECRET once the GitLab push
# webhook is wired up, so a missing secret fails the deploy rather than
# silently never syncing on push.
GTD_REQUIRED_VARS=GTD_REMOTE_URL GTD_PUSH_TOKEN GTD_ALLOWED_USERS GTD_WEBHOOK_SECRET

# Backstop git sync interval, seconds. 0 leaves the webhook as the only path.
#GTD_POLL_SECONDS=900

# Commit authorship.
#GTD_GIT_NAME=GTD MCP
#GTD_GIT_EMAIL=gtd-mcp@noreply
